  "worked_solution": "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+z & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(xz)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ xz & =x+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{x+C}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{x+C}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{x+C}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition  $y(1)=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =\\frac{1}{1+C}\\,\u27f9\\,C=-\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{(x-\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5x}{(5x-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
